EXAM_DATA.papers["2022-english-one"] = {"id":"2022-english-one","year":2022,"label":"2022 英语一","era":"modern","sourceUrl":"https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/kaoyan/paper/2022-english-one/","timeLimitMin":180,"scoreMode":"estimated","parts":[{"id":"section1","kind":"cloze","title":"Section I 完形填空","directions":"Directions: Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET . (10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section1-1","text":"The idea that plants have some degree of consciousness first took root in the early 2000s; the term \"plant neurobiology\" was   {{1}}   around the notion that some aspects of plant behavior could be   {{2}}   to intelligence in animals.   {{3}}   plants lack brains, the firing of electrical signals in their stems and leaves nonetheless triggered responses that   {{4}}   consciousness, researchers previously reported."},{"id":"p-section1-2","text":"But such an idea is untrue, according to a new opinion article. Plant biology is complex and fascinating, but it   {{5}}   so greatly from that of animals that so-called   {{6}}   of plants' intelligence is inconclusive, the authors wrote."},{"id":"p-section1-3","text":"Beginning in 2006, some scientists have   {{7}}   that plants possess neuron-like cells that interact with hormones and neurotransmitters,   {{8}}   \"a plant nervous system,   {{9}}   to that in animals,\" said lead study author Lincoln Taiz. \"They   {{10}}   claimed that plants have 'brain-like command centers' at their root tips.\""},{"id":"p-section1-4","text":"This   {{11}}   makes sense if you simplify the workings of a complex brain,   {{12}}   it to an array of electrical pulses; cells in plants also communicate through electrical signals.   {{13}}   , the signaling in a plant is only   {{14}}   similar to the firing in a complex animal brain, which is more than \"a mass of cells that communicate by electricity,\" Taiz said."},{"id":"p-section1-5","text":"\"For consciousness to evolve, a brain with a threshold   {{15}}   of complexity and capacity is required,\" he   {{16}}   . \"Since plants don't have nervous systems, the   {{17}}   that they have consciousness are effectively zero.\""},{"id":"p-section1-6","text":"And what's so great about consciousness, anyway? Plants can't run away from   {{18}}   , so investing energy in a body system which   {{19}}   a threat and can feel pain would be a very   {{20}}   evolutionary strategy, according to the article."}]},"questions":[{"n":1,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"coined"},{"key":"B","text":"discovered"},{"key":"C","text":"collected"},{"key":"D","text":"issued"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"1.","reason":"主语是 the term（一个新名称） 线索就在本句主语：the term \"plant neurobiology\" was __(1)__。主语是一个被引号标出的新造名称，动作应表「（名词/术语）被创造、被命名」，指向一个专门用于「造词」的动词。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"coin a term/word 是固定说法，意为「创造、新造（词语）」，与主语 the term \"plant neurobiology\" 严丝合缝。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"discover 是「发现」本已存在的事物（如新行星、新物种）；术语是人为创造的语言符号，不能说被「发现」。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"collect 是「收集、汇集」一批东西，与单个新造术语不搭。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"issue 多指「发布（声明、命令、刊物）」，对象是正式文书，不用于「造词」。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":2,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"attributed"},{"key":"B","text":"directed"},{"key":"C","text":"compared"},{"key":"D","text":"confined"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"2.","reason":"compare A to B = 把A比作B 本句说植物行为的某些方面 could be __(2)__ to intelligence in animals——把植物行为与「动物的智能」相提并论。语境要的是「比作、类比」，且后接 to。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"attribute A to B 意为「把A归因/归属于B」，强调因果或归属，而非「相提并论」，语义不合。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"direct ... to 指「把……引向」某处，与「把植物行为类比为动物智能」不沾边。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"compare A to B 正是「把A比作B」，与本句「植物行为某些方面可比作动物智能」完全吻合，呼应下文反复出现的 similar、analogous 等类比线索。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"confine ... to 意为「把……局限于」，表范围限制，与「类比」相反。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":3,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Unless"},{"key":"B","text":"When"},{"key":"C","text":"Once"},{"key":"D","text":"Though"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"3.","reason":"主句有 nonetheless，标志让步转折 主句里的 nonetheless triggered responses（nonetheless = 尽管如此、仍然）是铁证：前半「植物没有大脑」与后半「却仍触发了反应」构成让步转折，从句须用「尽管」类连词。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"unless 引导否定条件「除非……否则不」，与主句 nonetheless 的让步呼应不上。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"when「当……时」是时间关系，无法解释主句为何要用「却仍然（nonetheless）」这种对抗语气。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"once「一旦」引导条件，同样与 nonetheless 的让步逻辑不匹配。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"though「尽管」引导让步从句，与主句 nonetheless 一前一后构成标准让步转折：尽管植物没有大脑，电信号却仍触发了反应。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":4,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"coped with"},{"key":"B","text":"consisted of"},{"key":"C","text":"hinted at"},{"key":"D","text":"extended to"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"4.","reason":"全文立场：植物意识只是「迹象」非定论 线索靠全局：第2段 so-called __(6)__ of plants' intelligence is inconclusive（关于植物智能的证据并无定论）。作者认为电信号触发的反应顶多「暗示、暗指」意识，而非确凿等同。故该填一个表「暗示」的弱化动词。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"cope with「应付、处理（困难）」，主语通常是人或系统，「反应应对意识」语义不通。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"consist of「由……构成」，意为反应由意识组成，主谓宾关系荒唐，不合逻辑。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"hint at「暗示、暗指」，正合作者口吻：电信号触发的反应「暗示了」意识的存在——既转述了旧观点的推论，又留有余地、不下死论。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"extend to「延伸、扩展到」，表范围蔓延，「反应延伸到意识」搭配生硬、语义不准。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":5,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"suffers"},{"key":"B","text":"benefits"},{"key":"C","text":"develops"},{"key":"D","text":"differs"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"5.","reason":"But + 强调差异的语境 本句以 But 起、紧扣全文反方主线：Plant biology is complex and fascinating, but it __(5)__ so greatly from that of animals——植物生物学与动物的「差异如此之大」，正是作者否定类比的核心理由。语境要「相差、不同」。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"suffer from「遭受、患（病）」，主语是植物生物学「受动物之苦」毫无意义。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"benefit from「从……受益」，与作者强调「差异巨大故不可类比」的意图相反。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"develop「发展」此处接 from 既不构成本句要的固定表达，也无法表「与……不同」。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"differ from「与……不同」，配合 so greatly（如此之大）正表「植物生物学与动物的相差极大」，直接支撑下文「证据无定论」的结论。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":6,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"acceptance"},{"key":"B","text":"evidence"},{"key":"C","text":"cultivation"},{"key":"D","text":"creation"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"6.","reason":"表语 inconclusive 指向「证据」 线索是同句表语：so-called __(6)__ of plants' intelligence is inconclusive。inconclusive 意为「不确定的、无定论的」，能被这样评价的是「证据」——证据不足才会「无定论」。下文第6空 evidence 的语境也再次印证全文在谈「证据」是否成立。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"acceptance「接受、认可」是一种态度，说「接受是无定论的」逻辑不通。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"evidence「证据」与 inconclusive 是天然搭配：所谓植物智能的「证据」并不充分、无法定论——正是全篇否定旧观点的关键句。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"cultivation「栽培」蹭了 plant（植物）的字面联想，但「植物智能的栽培是无定论的」语义荒谬，是形义双重陷阱。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"creation「创造（物）」与 inconclusive 不搭，无法表「证据是否成立」之意。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":7,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"doubted"},{"key":"B","text":"denied"},{"key":"C","text":"argued"},{"key":"D","text":"requested"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"7.","reason":"下文 claimed 同义复现 线索在第3段后文：They __(10)__ claimed that plants have 'brain-like command centers'。同一批科学家后面用了 claimed（声称），可知第7空也是「主张、认为」类正向陈述，而非「怀疑/否认」。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"doubt「怀疑」与下文 claimed（声称拥有）方向相反——这批科学家是支持方，不会怀疑自己的主张。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"deny「否认」同样与「主张植物有神经样细胞」矛盾，立场颠倒。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"argue「主张、论证」后接 that 从句，正表「（某些科学家）认为植物拥有神经样细胞」，与下文 claimed 一脉相承。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"request「请求、要求」其后从句多用虚拟语气表请求，「请求植物拥有细胞」语义不通。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":8,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"adapting"},{"key":"B","text":"forming"},{"key":"C","text":"repairing"},{"key":"D","text":"testing"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"8.","reason":"前因后果：互动从而「构成」一个系统 线索在本句因果链：神经样细胞 interact with hormones and neurotransmitters, __(8)__ \"a plant nervous system\"——细胞与激素、神经递质相互作用，「形成」一个植物神经系统。语境要「形成、构成」。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"adapt「使适应、改编」，「适应一个神经系统」语义不顺，且无「产生」之意。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"form「形成、构成」正合本句：细胞与激素互动，从而「构成」一个植物神经系统，与引号内的整体名词搭配自然。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"repair「修理、修复」需有一个已存在且受损的对象，此处是「（凭空）形成」系统，逻辑不符。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"test「测试、检验」，「测试一个神经系统」与本句「互动产生系统」的因果不合。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":9,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"analogous"},{"key":"B","text":"essential"},{"key":"C","text":"suitable"},{"key":"D","text":"sensitive"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"9.","reason":"全文主线是「植物与动物作类比」 线索是全文反复出现的类比脉络：第1段 could be __(2)__ to intelligence in animals、第4段 the signaling in a plant is only __(14)__ similar to the firing in a complex animal brain。此处支持方说植物神经系统「类似于」动物的，要一个表「类似」且接 to 的形容词。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"analogous to「与……类似/可类比的」，与全文「植物 vs 动物」的类比主题严丝合缝，且 analogous 后正接 to。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"essential to「对……至关重要」，说植物神经系统「对动物神经系统必不可少」逻辑不通。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"suitable「适合的」与「类似」无关，「适合于动物神经系统」语义跑偏。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"sensitive to「对……敏感」，与本句要表的「相似」毫无关系。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":10,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"just"},{"key":"B","text":"ever"},{"key":"C","text":"still"},{"key":"D","text":"even"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"10.","reason":"本句主张比上句更夸张，构成递进 线索是相邻两句的程度递进：上句说植物有 a plant nervous system，本句进而说植物有 'brain-like command centers' at their root tips（脑样指挥中枢）——后者比前者更惊人，需用「甚至」来标示这种加码。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"just「仅仅、只不过」是弱化语气，与本句「主张更进一步、更夸张」的递进相反。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"ever「曾经、究竟」一般用于疑问、否定或比较句，肯定陈述句直接修饰 claimed 不地道。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"still「仍然、还」强调持续，本句是「进一步加码主张」而非「持续主张」，语气不准。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"even「甚至」恰好标出递进：他们「甚至」声称植物有脑样指挥中枢——比上句「有神经系统」更进一层，语气到位。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":11,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"restriction"},{"key":"B","text":"experiment"},{"key":"C","text":"perspective"},{"key":"D","text":"demand"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"11.","reason":"This 回指第3段那套「类比看法」 线索是 This 的指代：它回指第3段支持方提出的 \"a plant nervous system, __(9)__ to that in animals\" 那一整套观点/看法。这是一种「观点、视角」，故选 perspective。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"restriction「限制、约束」与前文那套「类比看法」无关。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"experiment「实验」指具体的实证操作，而 This 回指的是一种主张/解读，不是某次实验。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"perspective「观点、视角」恰好概括第3段那套「把植物类比为动物神经/大脑」的看法，且与后文「if you simplify...」（如果简化大脑则说得通）逻辑衔接。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"demand「要求、需求」与前文那套观点不是一回事。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":12,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"attaching"},{"key":"B","text":"reducing"},{"key":"C","text":"returning"},{"key":"D","text":"exposing"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"12.","reason":"前句 simplify 同义提示 线索在同句前半：if you simplify the workings of a complex brain, __(12)__ it to an array of electrical pulses。simplify（简化）是关键——把大脑「简化、降格」为一组电脉冲。reduce ... to 正是「把……简化/归结为」，与 simplify 同义。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"attach ... to「把……附着/连接到」，与「简化、降格」之意无关。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"reduce ... to「把……简化/归结为」，与前句 simplify 同义复现，把复杂大脑「降格为」一组电脉冲，搭配与语义双中。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"return ... to「使返回、归还」，与「简化为」无关。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"expose ... to「使……暴露/接触于」，与本句「简化、归结」之意不符。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":13,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"However"},{"key":"B","text":"Moreover"},{"key":"C","text":"Therefore"},{"key":"D","text":"Otherwise"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"13.","reason":"上句让步、本句转折拆穿 线索在前后句的转折关系：上文刚承认 This __(11)__ makes sense if you simplify the workings of a complex brain（这种观点在简化大脑后说得通），本句却说 the signaling in a plant is only __(14)__ similar to the firing in a complex animal brain（植物的信号只是表面上像）——先扬后抑，是典型转折，须用 However。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"However「然而」正合本处转折：上句承认类比表面成立，本句立刻指出植物信号「只是表面相似」，转折拆穿，呼应全文反方立场。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"Moreover「此外、而且」表同向递进，但上下句是「认可→否定」的对立，不是叠加。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Therefore「因此」表前因后果，但本句不是上句的结论，而是对它的反驳。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"Otherwise「否则、不然」引出相反假设的后果，与本处「然而事实是」的转折不符。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":14,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"temporarily"},{"key":"B","text":"literally"},{"key":"C","text":"superficially"},{"key":"D","text":"imaginarily"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"14.","reason":"only + 下文「不止是电活动」共指「表面」 线索是 only 与下文的对比：本句 only __(14)__ similar（只是某种程度相似），下文紧接 which is more than \"a mass of cells that communicate by electricity\"（大脑远不止靠电交流的一堆细胞）。作者要说植物信号与大脑只是「表面相似」，深层并不同——故选 superficially。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"temporarily「暂时地」强调时间短暂，本句讲的是相似的「深浅」而非时间，方向不对。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"literally「真正地、字面上」是强化相似，与 only 及作者「实则不同」的否定立场恰好相反。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"superficially「表面上、肤浅地」配合 only，正表「只是表面相似」，与下文「大脑远不止靠电交流」的深层差异形成对照，贴合反方立场。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"imaginarily「凭想象地」生僻且不合句意——植物信号与大脑放电的相似是客观表面现象，并非纯属想象。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":15,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"list"},{"key":"B","text":"level"},{"key":"C","text":"label"},{"key":"D","text":"load"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"15.","reason":"of complexity and capacity 指向「程度」 线索是空后的 of 短语：a threshold __(15)__ of complexity and capacity——complexity（复杂度）、capacity（容量）都是可有「高低程度」的量，需一个表「水平、程度」的名词。a level of complexity（复杂程度）是地道搭配。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"list of「……的清单」，「复杂度的清单」语义不通。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"level「水平、程度」，a threshold level of complexity and capacity「一个临界的复杂度与容量水平」，搭配与语义俱佳，正合「意识演化需要大脑达到某种复杂度门槛」之意。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"label「标签、标记」，「复杂度的标签」不通，与「程度」无关。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"load「负载、负荷」虽含量的意味，但 load of complexity 不是惯用搭配，且不如 level 准确表「水平/程度」。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":16,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"recalled"},{"key":"B","text":"agreed"},{"key":"C","text":"questioned"},{"key":"D","text":"added"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"16.","reason":"Taiz 在前文基础上再补一句 线索是说话人连贯性：Taiz 是本文主述者，第4段已引述其观点，第5段他又给出新论断 For consciousness to evolve, a brain with a threshold __(15)__ of complexity and capacity is required——这是在原有论述上「补充」新内容，故 added（补充说）最贴。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"recall「回忆、追述」需指向过去经历，本处是当下给出论断，并非回忆往事。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"agree「同意」要有前面某人的观点供其附和，但此处是 Taiz 自己提出新论点，无附和对象。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"question「质疑」表怀疑语气，但 Taiz 此处是肯定地陈述自己的论断，并非质疑。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"add「补充说」正合语境：Taiz 在前文论述之上又补一条——意识演化需要大脑达到复杂度门槛，自然衔接。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":17,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"chances"},{"key":"B","text":"risks"},{"key":"C","text":"excuses"},{"key":"D","text":"assumptions"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"17.","reason":"Since 因果 + zero 指向「可能性」 线索在同句因果：Since plants don't have nervous systems, the __(17)__ that they have consciousness are effectively zero——既然植物没有神经系统，那它们有意识的「可能性」几乎为零。chances（可能性）与 zero 天然相配。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"chances「可能性、机会」与 effectively zero 严丝合缝：植物有意识的「可能性」几乎为零，正是第5段的结论句。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"risk「风险」带负面色彩，「植物有意识的风险为零」语义别扭——有意识并非「风险」。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"excuse「借口、理由」，「植物有意识的借口为零」逻辑不通。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"assumption「假设」是可数的具体命题，说「假设几乎为零」不合逻辑——假设要么有要么无，不论「为零」。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":18,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"danger"},{"key":"B","text":"failure"},{"key":"C","text":"warning"},{"key":"D","text":"control"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"18.","reason":"下文 threat、feel pain 同指「危险」 线索在下文复现：investing energy in a body system which __(19)__ a threat and can feel pain——下文出现 threat（威胁）、feel pain（感到疼痛）。植物无法逃离的、且与威胁/疼痛同类的，正是 danger（危险）。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"danger「危险」既与 run away from 自然搭配（逃离危险），又与下文 threat、pain 同类互证：植物无法逃离危险，故感知威胁、感到疼痛的系统对它无用。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"failure「失败」与下文 threat、pain 不同类，「逃离失败」也不合本句逻辑。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"warning「警告」是危险的信号而非危险本身，「逃离警告」不准；且与下文 pain 不同类。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"control「控制」与「逃跑、威胁、疼痛」的语义场不符。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":19,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"represents"},{"key":"B","text":"includes"},{"key":"C","text":"reveals"},{"key":"D","text":"recognizes"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"19.","reason":"并列 can feel pain 提示「感知」 线索是并列项：a body system which __(19)__ a threat and can feel pain。后半「能感到疼痛」是一种感知能力，前半与之并列，对 threat 也应是「识别、感知」——recognizes（识别）才与 feel pain 同属感知功能。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"represent「代表、象征」，「身体系统代表一个威胁」与「感到疼痛」不同类，逻辑跑偏。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"include「包含」，「系统包含一个威胁」语义不通，也非感知动作。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"reveal「揭示、显露」是向外暴露信息，而本句要的是系统「察觉」威胁这一向内的感知动作，方向相反。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"recognize「识别、察觉」，与并列的 can feel pain 同为感知功能：一个能识别威胁、能感到疼痛的身体系统，逻辑协调。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":20,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"humble"},{"key":"B","text":"poor"},{"key":"C","text":"practical"},{"key":"D","text":"easy"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"20.","reason":"前文 can't run away → 该策略没用、糟糕 线索是同句因果：Plants can't run away from __(18)__, so investing energy in a body system which __(19)__ a threat and can feel pain would be a very __(20)__ evolutionary strategy——植物逃不掉，故为「感知威胁、感受疼痛」的系统投入能量是「很糟糕」的策略。so 引出贬义结论，需 poor（糟糕的）。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"humble「谦卑的、不起眼的」，评价一个策略「谦逊」不知所云，色彩也不对。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"poor「糟糕的、拙劣的」，正合 so 引出的负面结论：对逃不掉的植物来说，投资能感知威胁、感受疼痛的系统是「很糟糕」的演化策略。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"practical「实用的」是褒义，与作者「这种策略毫无意义」的贬抑相反，正话反说陷阱。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"easy「容易的」评价的是难度而非优劣，与本句要表的「糟糕、不划算」不在一个维度。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-1","kind":"reading","title":"Text 1","textNo":1,"directions":"Directions: Read the following four texts. Answer the questions after each text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET . (40 points)","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 1","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-1","text":"People often complain that plastics are too durable. Water bottles, shopping bags, and other trash litter the planet, from Mount Everest to the Mariana Trench, because plastics are everywhere and don't break down easily. But some plastic materials change over time. They crack and frizzle. They \"weep\" out additives. They melt into sludge. All of which creates huge headaches for institutions, such as museums, trying to preserve culturally important objects. The variety of plastic objects at risk is dizzying: early radios, avant-garde sculptures, celluloid animation stills from Disney films, the first artificial heart."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-2","text":"Certain artifacts are especially vulnerable because some pioneers in plastic art didn't always know how to mix ingredients properly, says Thea van Oosten, a polymer chemist who, until retiring a few years ago, worked for decades at the Cultural Heritage Agency of the Netherlands (RCE). \"It's like baking a cake: If you don't have exact amounts, it goes wrong,\" she says. \"The object you make is already a time bomb.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-3","text":"And sometimes, it's not the artist's fault. In the 1960s, the Italian artist Piero Gilardi began to create hundreds of bright, colorful foam pieces. Those pieces included small beds of roses and other items as well as a few dozen \"nature carpets\"—large rectangles decorated with foam pumpkins, cabbages, and watermelons. He wanted viewers to walk around on the carpets—which meant they had to be durable."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-4","text":"Unfortunately, the polyurethane foam he used is inherently unstable. It's especially vulnerable to light damage, and by the mid-1990s, Gilardi's pumpkins, roses, and other figures were splitting and crumbling. Museums locked some of them away in the dark."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-5","text":"So van Oosten and colleagues worked to preserve Gilardi's sculptures. They infused some with stabilizing and consolidating chemicals. Van Oosten calls those chemicals \"sunscreens\" because their goal was to prevent further light damage and rebuild worn polymer fibers. She is proud that several sculptures have even gone on display again, albeit sometimes beneath protective cases."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-6","text":"Despite success stories like van Oosten's, preservation of plastics will likely get harder. Old objects continue to deteriorate. Worse, biodegradable plastics, designed to disintegrate, are increasingly common."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-7","text":"And more is at stake here than individual objects. Joana Lia Ferreira, an assistant professor of conservation and restoration at the NOVA School of Science and Technology, notes that archaeologists first defined the great material ages of human history—Stone Age, Iron Age, and so on—after examining artifacts in museums. We now live in an age of plastic, she says, \"and what we decide to collect today, what we decide to preserve…will have a strong impact on how in the future we'll be seen.\""}]},"questions":[{"n":21,"stem":"According to Paragraph 1, museums are faced with difficulties in____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"maintaining their plastic items."},{"key":"B","text":"obtaining durable plastic artifacts."},{"key":"C","text":"handling outdated plastic exhibits."},{"key":"D","text":"classifying their plastic collections."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据第一段,博物馆在____方面面临困难","choicesZh":{"A":"维护它们的塑料物品","B":"获得耐用的塑料文物","C":"处理过时的塑料展品","D":"对它们的塑料藏品进行分类"},"analysis":{"type":"According to Paragraph 1, museums are faced with difficulties in 给出明确段号，问的是具体困难的对象，典型细节填空。","parse":"museums are faced with difficulties in____ 是被动语态，with 引出对象、in 引出难处所在领域；空格处需要填动名词短语，提示选项均以 -ing 起首。","locateEn":"All of which creates huge headaches for institutions, such as museums, trying to preserve culturally important objects.","locateZh":"所有这些（塑料的老化变化）给那些试图保存具有文化重要性的物品的机构（例如博物馆）带来了巨大的麻烦。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判的「保存塑料藏品」在 A 选项中以 maintaining their plastic items 同义改写完整呈现，与定位句严丝合缝。","pairs":[{"orig":"trying to preserve culturally important objects","opt":"maintaining their plastic items"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"obtaining durable plastic artifacts 说的是获得耐用塑料文物，原文谈的是博物馆已经有藏品但难以保存，方向颠倒。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"handling outdated plastic exhibits 把问题归到塑料展品「过时」，但原文谈的是物理老化、化学退化，并未涉及风格或潮流过时。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"classifying their plastic collections 强调把藏品归类，原文从未提及分类困难，纯属编造。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":22,"stem":"Van Oosten believes that certain plastic objects are____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"immune to decay."},{"key":"B","text":"improperly shaped."},{"key":"C","text":"inherently flawed."},{"key":"D","text":"complex in structure."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"Van Oosten认为某些塑料物品____","choicesZh":{"A":"不受腐蚀影响","B":"形状不当","C":"存在固有缺陷","D":"结构复杂"},"analysis":{"type":"Van Oosten believes that certain plastic objects are____ 问的是该人物对一个具体对象的判断，直接落在原文引语里。","parse":"Van Oosten believes that certain plastic objects are____ 是主谓宾从句结构，that 从句中表语空缺，预期填形容词短语。","locateEn":"\"It's like baking a cake: If you don't have exact amounts, it goes wrong,\" she says. \"The object you make is already a time bomb.\"","locateZh":"她说：“这就像烤蛋糕，如果你的用量不准，就会出错。”“你做出来的物件本身就已经是一颗定时炸弹了。”","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"immune to decay 意为不会衰变，但原文恰恰说 goes wrong 与 time bomb，方向完全相反。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"improperly shaped 把缺陷归到形状不当，但原文谈的是 mix ingredients properly 即配料比例不当，与形状无关。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判的「出厂即缺陷」在 C 选项中以 inherently flawed 这一抽象表述完整命中，比喻被还原为形容词短语。","pairs":[{"orig":"The object you make is already a time bomb","opt":"inherently flawed"},{"orig":"If you don't have exact amounts, it goes wrong","opt":"inherently flawed"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"complex in structure 强调结构复杂，原文从未提到塑料的结构复杂度，仅谈成分比例与稳定性。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":23,"stem":"Museums stopped exhibiting some of Gilardi's artworks to____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"keep them from hurting visitors."},{"key":"B","text":"duplicate them for future display."},{"key":"C","text":"have their ingredients analyzed."},{"key":"D","text":"prevent them from further damage."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"博物馆停止展出Gilardi的一些艺术品是为了____","choicesZh":{"A":"防止它们伤害参观者","B":"复制它们以供未来展出","C":"对它们的成分进行分析","D":"防止它们进一步损坏"},"analysis":{"type":"Museums stopped exhibiting some of Gilardi's artworks to____ 用不定式问目的，目的即原文给出的具体原因，属细节题。","parse":"Museums stopped exhibiting some of Gilardi's artworks to____ 主干为 Museums stopped exhibiting...，不定式作目的状语，空格处填动词原形短语。","locateEn":"It's especially vulnerable to light damage, and by the mid-1990s, Gilardi's pumpkins, roses, and other figures were splitting and crumbling. Museums locked some of them away in the dark.","locateZh":"这种聚氨酯泡沫特别容易受光损伤，到 1990 年代中期，Gilardi 的南瓜、玫瑰等造型已经开裂、碎裂。博物馆于是把其中一些锁进了暗处。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"keep them from hurting visitors 把目的说成保护观众，但原文从未提到泡沫雕塑会伤人，且锁起来的明确原因是材料脆弱。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"duplicate them for future display 强调以后再展出，原文只提「锁起来」，未提复制或为未来展出做准备。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"have their ingredients analyzed 描述的是 Van Oosten 在第 5 段 infused some with stabilizing and consolidating chemicals 的修复行动，且与「锁起来」动作不是一回事；同时博物馆并未被原文写成做成分分析的主体。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判的「避免光照继续损坏」在 D 选项中以 prevent them from further damage 完整命中。","pairs":[{"orig":"Museums locked some of them away in the dark","opt":"prevent them from further damage"},{"orig":"especially vulnerable to light damage","opt":"prevent them from further damage"}]}}}}},{"n":24,"stem":"The author thinks that preservation of plastics is____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"costly."},{"key":"B","text":"unworthy."},{"key":"C","text":"unpopular."},{"key":"D","text":"challenging."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"作者认为塑料的保存工作____","choicesZh":{"A":"代价高昂","B":"不值得","C":"不受欢迎","D":"具有挑战性"},"analysis":{"type":"The author thinks that preservation of plastics is____ 问的是作者对一个具体对象的评价，选项均为程度形容词，定位句直接命中。","parse":"The author thinks that preservation of plastics is____ 主从复合句，that 从句中表语空缺，预期填形容词。","locateEn":"Despite success stories like van Oosten's, preservation of plastics will likely get harder.","locateZh":"尽管有像 Van Oosten 这样的成功案例，塑料保存工作仍然可能变得越来越棘手。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"costly 说花钱，原文从未提到保存塑料的经济成本，纯粹是常识联想。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"unworthy 暗示保存塑料不值得，但作者讲了 She is proud that several sculptures have even gone on display again 与末段 will have a strong impact on how in the future we'll be seen，明确肯定其价值。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"unpopular 谈的是受欢迎程度，但原文没有评价塑料保存在公众或机构中的流行度。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判的「越来越难」在 D 中以 challenging 直接同义命中，且与作者让步语气一致。","pairs":[{"orig":"preservation of plastics will likely get harder","opt":"challenging"},{"orig":"Old objects continue to deteriorate","opt":"challenging"}]}}}}},{"n":25,"stem":"In Ferreira's opinion, preservation of plastic artifacts____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"will inspire future scientific research."},{"key":"B","text":"has profound historical significance."},{"key":"C","text":"will help us separate the material ages."},{"key":"D","text":"has an impact on today's cultural life."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"在Ferreira看来,塑料文物的保存____","choicesZh":{"A":"将激发未来的科学研究","B":"具有深远的历史意义","C":"将帮助我们区分物质时代","D":"对当今的文化生活有影响"},"analysis":{"type":"In Ferreira's opinion, preservation of plastic artifacts____ 问 Ferreira 对该对象的看法，原文有其直接引语支撑。","parse":"In Ferreira's opinion, preservation of plastic artifacts____ In Ferreira's opinion 是介词短语作状语，主干主语是 preservation of plastic artifacts，谓语整体空缺。","locateEn":"We now live in an age of plastic, she says, and what we decide to collect today, what we decide to preserve…will have a strong impact on how in the future we'll be seen.","locateZh":"她说，我们如今生活在塑料时代，我们今天决定收藏什么、决定保存什么，将对未来人如何看待我们产生深远影响。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"will inspire future scientific research 说会启发未来科研，原文只谈历史评价与文物收藏，未涉及科研启示。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判的「决定后人如何看我们」在 B 选项中以 has profound historical significance 概括到位。","pairs":[{"orig":"archaeologists first defined the great material ages of human history","opt":"has profound historical significance"},{"orig":"will have a strong impact on how in the future we'll be seen","opt":"has profound historical significance"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"will help us separate the material ages 把「区分材料时代」作为未来动作，但原文 archaeologists first defined the great material ages 是已发生的事，且 Ferreira 用它做类比而非目标。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"has an impact on today's cultural life 把影响对象换成「今天的文化生活」，但原文 how in the future we'll be seen 明确指向未来后人对我们的评价，时序刚好相反。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-2","kind":"reading","title":"Text 2","textNo":2,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 2","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-1","text":"As the latest crop of students pen their undergraduate application form and weigh up their options, it may be worth considering just how the point, purpose and value of a degree has changed and what Generation Z need to consider as they start the third stage of their educational journey."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-2","text":"Millennials were told that if you did well in school, got a decent degree, you would be set up for life. But that promise has been found wanting. As degrees became universal, they became devalued. Education was no longer a secure route of social mobility. Today, 28 per cent of graduates in the UK are in non-graduate roles, a percentage which is double the average among the OECD countries."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-3","text":"This is not to say that there is no point in getting a degree, but rather stress that a degree is not for everyone, that the switch from classroom to lecture hall is not an inevitable one and that other options are available."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-4","text":"Thankfully, there are signs that this is already happening, with Generation Z seeking to learn from their millennial predecessors, even if parents and teachers tend to be still set in the degree mindset. Employers have long seen the advantages of hiring school leavers who often prove themselves to be more committed and loyal employees than graduates. Many too are seeing the advantages of scrapping a degree requirement for certain roles."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-5","text":"For those for whom a degree is the desired route, consider that this may well be the first of many. In this age of generalists, it pays to have specific knowledge or skills. Postgraduates now earn 40 per cent more than graduates. When more and more of us have a degree, it makes sense to have two."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-6","text":"It is unlikely that Generation Z will be done with education at 18 or 21; they will need to be constantly upskilling throughout their career to stay employable. It has been estimated that this generation, due to the pressures of technology, the wish for personal fulfilment and desire for diversity, will work for 17 different employers over the course of their working life and have five different careers. Education, and not just knowledge gained on campus, will be a core part of Generation Z's career trajectory."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-7","text":"Older generations often talk about their degree in the present and personal tense: 'I am a geographer' or 'I am a classicist'. Their sons or daughters would never say such a thing; it's as if they already know that their degree won't define them in the same way."}]},"questions":[{"n":26,"stem":"The author suggests that Generation Z should____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"be careful in choosing a college."},{"key":"B","text":"be diligent at each educational stage."},{"key":"C","text":"reassess the necessity of college education."},{"key":"D","text":"postpone their undergraduate application."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"作者建议Z世代应该____","choicesZh":{"A":"在选择大学时要谨慎。","B":"在每个教育阶段都要勤奋。","C":"重新评估大学教育的必要性。","D":"推迟他们的本科申请。"},"analysis":{"type":"The author suggests that Generation Z should 问的不是某句明示细节，而是作者通篇想给 Z 世代的总建议；答案须覆盖全文立场走向，属主旨题（作者主张类）。","parse":"The author suggests that Generation Z should____ 是 suggest + that 宾语从句结构，主句主语是 The author，从句主语 Generation Z 后接情态动词 should + 空缺动词原形；选项是动词短语，作 should 之后的谓语，无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"全文未涉及如何在多所大学之间挑选，be careful in choosing a college 是文中没有讨论过的动作。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"文中讨论的是是否需要学位，be diligent at each educational stage 把话题偷换成各教育阶段的学习态度，与作者主张不在同一层面。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判'重新审视大学必要性'与 reassess the necessity of college education 严丝合缝，覆盖第 3 段否定式表态及全文走向。","pairs":[{"orig":"a degree is not for everyone","opt":"reassess the necessity of college education"},{"orig":"other options are available","opt":"reassess the necessity of college education"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"作者并未让 Z 世代 postpone their undergraduate application，P1 只是说他们填申请时应思考学位的意义，谈的是反思而非推迟。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":27,"stem":"The percentage of UK graduates in non-graduate roles reflects____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Millennial's opinion about work."},{"key":"B","text":"the shrinking value of a degree."},{"key":"C","text":"public discontent with education."},{"key":"D","text":"the desired route of social mobility."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"英国毕业生从事非毕业生岗位的比例反映了____","choicesZh":{"A":"千禧一代对工作的看法。","B":"学位价值的缩水。","C":"公众对教育的不满。","D":"社会流动的理想途径。"},"analysis":{"type":"The percentage of UK graduates in non-graduate roles reflects____ 是补全句，问这个具体数据反映了什么客观事实，属于细节信息的转述。","parse":"The percentage of UK graduates in non-graduate roles reflects____ 主语是 The percentage，带两个 of 短语修饰（of UK graduates 与 in non-graduate roles），谓语 reflects 后接空缺名词宾语；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"But that promise has been found wanting. As degrees became universal, they became devalued. Education was no longer a secure route of social mobility. Today, 28 per cent of graduates in the UK are in non-graduate roles, a percentage which is double the average among the OECD countries.","locateZh":"但这一承诺已被发现落空。随着学位变得普及，它们也随之贬值。教育不再是社会流动的稳妥途径。如今，英国 28% 的毕业生在做非毕业生岗位，这一比例是 OECD 国家平均值的两倍。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"数据反映的是学位贬值这一客观事实，与 Millennial's opinion about work 即千禧一代对工作的看法无关；千禧一代在文中是被许诺的对象，不是这个百分比所反映的主体。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判'学位贬值'与 the shrinking value of a degree 表层和深层都吻合，是 they became devalued 的同义改写。","pairs":[{"orig":"they became devalued","opt":"the shrinking value of a degree"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"public discontent with education 是公众对教育的不满，原文未涉及公众舆论或情绪。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文 Education was no longer a secure route of social mobility 否定教育是社会流动的稳妥途径，the desired route of social mobility 反而把它说成是被向往的路线，与原文方向相反。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":28,"stem":"The author considers it a good sign that____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Generation Z are seeking to earn a decent degree."},{"key":"B","text":"school leavers are willing to be skilled workers."},{"key":"C","text":"employers are taking a realistic attitude to degree."},{"key":"D","text":"parents are changing their minds about education."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"作者认为____是一个好迹象","choicesZh":{"A":"Z世代正在寻求获得一个体面的学位。","B":"离校生愿意成为技术工人。","C":"雇主对学位采取现实的态度。","D":"父母正在改变他们对教育的看法。"},"analysis":{"type":"The author considers it a good sign that____ 直接问作者把哪件具体事情视为好兆头，答案在原文表态句里有直接对应，不是推断。","parse":"The author considers it a good sign that____ 用 consider + it + 评价短语 + that 从句的形式宾语结构，it 替指 that 从句；选项作 that 从句的完整命题。结构常见，无陷阱。","locateEn":"Employers have long seen the advantages of hiring school leavers who often prove themselves to be more committed and loyal employees than graduates. Many too are seeing the advantages of scrapping a degree requirement for certain roles.","locateZh":"雇主们早已看到雇用高中毕业生的好处——他们往往比大学毕业生更敬业、更忠诚；许多雇主也开始看到取消某些岗位学位要求的好处。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Generation Z are seeking to earn a decent degree 与第 4 段方向相反，原文是 Z 世代正吸取教训不再盲目追求学位，且 get a decent degree 是千禧一代被许诺的旧路径。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文说雇主发现高中毕业生 more committed and loyal，未说高中毕业生 willing to be skilled workers，将雇主的发现偷换为高中毕业生的意愿。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判'雇主务实'与 employers are taking a realistic attitude to degree 一一对应，选项把段内雇主的两个具体行为统一为对学位的务实态度。","pairs":[{"orig":"scrapping a degree requirement for certain roles","opt":"employers are taking a realistic attitude to degree"},{"orig":"Employers have long seen the advantages of hiring school leavers","opt":"employers are taking a realistic attitude to degree"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文 parents and teachers tend to be still set in the degree mindset 明示父母仍固守学位思维，parents are changing their minds about education 与之直接相反。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":29,"stem":"It is advised in Paragraph 5 that those with one degree should____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"make an early decision on their career."},{"key":"B","text":"attend on-the-job training programs."},{"key":"C","text":"team up with high-paid postgraduates."},{"key":"D","text":"further their studies in a specific field."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"第5段建议那些拥有一个学位的人应该____","choicesZh":{"A":"尽早决定他们的职业。","B":"参加在职培训项目。","C":"与高薪研究生合作。","D":"在某个特定领域深造。"},"analysis":{"type":"It is advised in Paragraph 5 that those with one degree should____ 是被动语态的建议陈述，问段内明示的建议，属于段落细节题。","parse":"It is advised in Paragraph 5 that those with one degree should____ 用 it 作形式主语、that 从句为真主语的被动句；those with one degree 是从句主语，should 后接空缺动词原形。结构常见，无陷阱。","locateEn":"In this age of generalists, it pays to have specific knowledge or skills. Postgraduates now earn 40 per cent more than graduates. When more and more of us have a degree, it makes sense to have two.","locateZh":"在通才横行的时代，掌握具体的知识或技能很值钱。研究生现在比本科生多挣 40%。当越来越多的人都有学位时，拥有两个学位就成了合理之举。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"make an early decision on their career 提早做职业决定一事文中未出现；第 6 段反而说 Z 世代会换 17 个雇主、5 种职业，方向甚至相反。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"attend on-the-job training programs 在岗培训项目与第 5 段所言'再读一个学位'不在一个范畴；段内未提及 on-the-job training。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文 Postgraduates now earn 40 per cent more than graduates 只是把研究生收入作论据，选项却把它扭曲成 team up with high-paid postgraduates 与高薪研究生组队，无原文支持。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判'在具体方向再深造'与 further their studies in a specific field 同时落在 specific field 与 further studies 两个要素上，是段内三句共同导向的唯一答案。","pairs":[{"orig":"specific knowledge or skills","opt":"further their studies in a specific field"},{"orig":"it makes sense to have two","opt":"further their studies"}]}}}}},{"n":30,"stem":"What can be concluded about Generation Z from the last two paragraphs?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Lifelong learning will define them."},{"key":"B","text":"They will make qualified educators."},{"key":"C","text":"Degrees will no longer appeal to them."},{"key":"D","text":"They will have a limited choice of jobs."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"推断题","stemZh":"从最后两段可以得出关于Z世代的什么结论?","choicesZh":{"A":"终身学习将定义他们。","B":"他们将成为合格的教育工作者。","C":"学位将不再吸引他们。","D":"他们的工作选择将会有限。"},"analysis":{"type":"What can be concluded about Generation Z from the last two paragraphs? 问的是从末两段能得出什么结论，不是单句细节，需把两段信息汇总后推出。","parse":"What can be concluded about Generation Z from the last two paragraphs? 是 What 引导的特殊疑问句，被动语态 can be concluded；about 短语指对象、from 短语指来源；选项是完整陈述句作答案命题。结构清晰，无陷阱。","locateEn":"It is unlikely that Generation Z will be done with education at 18 or 21; they will need to be constantly upskilling throughout their career to stay employable.","locateZh":"Z 世代不太可能在 18 岁或 21 岁就结束教育；为了在整个职业生涯中保持受雇能力，他们需要不断提升技能。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判'终身学习定义这一代'与 Lifelong learning will define them 严丝合缝，是末两段唯一能同时覆盖两段主旨的选项。","pairs":[{"orig":"they will need to be constantly upskilling throughout their career to stay employable","opt":"Lifelong learning will define them"},{"orig":"their degree won't define them in the same way","opt":"Lifelong learning will define them"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文谈的是 Z 世代自己需要不断学习，They will make qualified educators 把对象偷换为他们将成为合格的教育者，与原文无关。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文只说 their degree won't define them in the same way，并未推出 Degrees will no longer appeal to them 学位不再吸引他们；'不以同样方式定义'≠'失去吸引力'。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文 work for 17 different employers 与 five different careers 表明 Z 世代职业选择丰富多样，They will have a limited choice of jobs 反而说他们工作选择有限，与原文方向相反。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-3","kind":"reading","title":"Text 3","textNo":3,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 3","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-1","text":"Enlightening, challenging, stimulating, fun. These were some of the words that Nature readers used to describe their experience of art-science collaborations in a series of articles on partnerships between artists and researchers. Nearly 40% of the roughly 350 people who responded to an accompanying poll said they had collaborated with artists; and almost all said they would consider doing so in future."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-2","text":"Such an encouraging result is not surprising. Scientists are increasingly seeking out visual artists to help them communicate their work to new audiences. \"Artists help scientists reach a broader audience and make emotional connections that enhance learning,\" one respondent said."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-3","text":"One example of how artists and scientists have together rocked the scenes came last month when the Sydney Symphony Orchestra performed a reworked version of Antonio Vivaldi's The Four Seasons. They reimagined the 300-year-old score by injecting the latest climate prediction data for each season—provided by Monash University's Climate Change Communication Research Hub. The performance was a creative call to action ahead of November's United Nations Climate Change Conference in Glasgow, UK."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-4","text":"But a genuine partnership must be a two-way street. Fewer artists than scientists responded to the Nature poll; however, several respondents noted that artists do not simply assist scientists with their communication requirements. Nor should their work be considered only as an object of study. The alliances are most valuable when scientists and artists have a shared stake in a project, are able to jointly design it and can critique each other's work. Such an approach can both prompt new research as well as result in powerful art."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-5","text":"More than half a century ago, the Massachusetts Institute of Technology opened its Center for Advanced Visual Studies (CAVS) to explore the role of technology in culture. The founders deliberately focused their projects around light—hence the \"visual studies\" in the name. Light was something that both artists and scientists had an interest in, and therefore could form the basis of collaboration. As science and technology progressed, and divided into more sub-disciplines, the centre was simultaneously looking to a time when leading researchers could also be artists, writers and poets, and vice versa."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-6","text":"Nature's poll findings suggest that this trend is as strong as ever, but, to make a collaboration work, both sides need to invest time, and embrace surprise and challenge. The reach of art-science tie-ups needs to go beyond the necessary purpose of research communication, and participants must not fall into the trap of stereotyping each other. Artists and scientists alike are immersed in discovery and invention, and challenge and critique are core to both, too."}]},"questions":[{"n":31,"stem":"According to Paragraph 1, art-science collaborations have____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"caught the attention of critics."},{"key":"B","text":"received favorable responses."},{"key":"C","text":"promoted academic publishing."},{"key":"D","text":"sparked heated public disputes."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据第一段,艺术与科学的合作已经____","choicesZh":{"A":"引起了批评者的注意。","B":"收到了积极的反响。","C":"促进了学术出版。","D":"引发了激烈的公众争议。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 According to Paragraph 1, art-science collaborations have____ 是对第 1 段一个客观事实的转述补全，不含 infer/suggest/imply 等推断词，也不问主旨或态度。","parse":"题干为待补全陈述句，主干是 art-science collaborations have____，have 后接动词原形或被动态的过去分词，由四个选项延续；According to Paragraph 1 为状语，提示出处。结构平铺、无陷阱。","locateEn":"Enlightening, challenging, stimulating, fun. These were some of the words that Nature readers used to describe their experience of art-science collaborations in a series of articles on partnerships between artists and researchers. Nearly 40% of the roughly 350 people who responded to an accompanying poll said they had collaborated with artists; and almost all said they would consider doing so in future.","locateZh":"启发性强、富有挑战、引人思考、有趣。这些是《自然》杂志读者在一系列关于艺术家与研究者合作的文章中用来描述其艺-科合作体验的一些词。在约 350 位回应配套民调的人里，近 40% 表示自己曾与艺术家合作；几乎所有人都表示未来愿意尝试合作。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"caught the attention of critics 把原文的 Nature readers（读者）偷换成 critics（评论家），主体不符；原文也没说合作\"引起关注\"，只说读者用褒义词描述了体验。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判要求选\"正面反馈\"，四项里只有 favorable responses 与之对应；其余三项都偏离了\"读者反馈\"这一定位句的核心信息。","pairs":[{"orig":"Enlightening, challenging, stimulating, fun","opt":"favorable responses"},{"orig":"almost all said they would consider doing so in future","opt":"received favorable responses"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"promoted academic publishing 中的 academic publishing（学术出版）在第 1 段没有任何对应——原文谈的是 Nature 杂志做的一组文章和民调，并未说艺-科合作推动了学术出版。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"sparked heated public disputes 的 disputes（争论、争议）是负面意义，与原文整段的褒义反馈方向相反；原文也无 public（公众）这一主体。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":32,"stem":"The reworked version of The Four Seasons is mentioned to show that____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"art can offer audiences easy access to science."},{"key":"B","text":"science can help with the expression of emotions."},{"key":"C","text":"public participation in science has a promising future."},{"key":"D","text":"art is effective in facilitating scientific innovations."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"提到改编版《四季》是为了表明____","choicesZh":{"A":"艺术可以为观众提供接触科学的便捷途径。","B":"科学可以帮助表达情感。","C":"公众参与科学有着光明的前景。","D":"艺术在促进科学创新方面是有效的。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The reworked version of The Four Seasons is mentioned to show that____ 中 \"is mentioned to show\" 明确询问举这个例子的目的，而非例子本身的细节。","parse":"题干主干 The reworked version ... is mentioned to show that____，不定式 to show 是 mentioned 的目的状语，that 引导宾语从句承接四个选项；要点是看清问的是\"为何提\"，不是\"提了什么\"。","locateEn":"One example of how artists and scientists have together rocked the scenes came last month when the Sydney Symphony Orchestra performed a reworked version of Antonio Vivaldi's The Four Seasons. They reimagined the 300-year-old score by injecting the latest climate prediction data for each season—provided by Monash University's Climate Change Communication Research Hub. The performance was a creative call to action ahead of November's United Nations Climate Change Conference in Glasgow, UK.","locateZh":"上个月，悉尼交响乐团演奏了维瓦尔第《四季》的改编版，这是艺术家和科学家共同震撼舞台的一个例子。他们把莫纳什大学气候变化传播研究中心提供的最新各季气候预测数据注入这部 300 年前的乐谱，重新构想了它。此次演出是 11 月格拉斯哥联合国气候变化大会前的一次极具创意的行动号召。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判要求\"艺术让科学走向大众\"，art can offer audiences easy access to science 完整覆盖 art / audience / science 三个核心要素及其方向。","pairs":[{"orig":"Artists help scientists reach a broader audience","opt":"art can offer audiences easy access to science"},{"orig":"make emotional connections that enhance learning","opt":"easy access to science"},{"orig":"injecting the latest climate prediction data","opt":"art ... access to science"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"science can help with the expression of emotions 把方向反过来——成了\"科学帮助情感表达\"；原文是艺术家借助情感连接帮科学传播，并未说科学服务于情感表达。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"public participation in science has a promising future 中的 public participation in science（公众参与科学）这一行为在第 2、3 段没有任何对应——原文谈的是公众作为受众接收科学，而非参与科学研究本身。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"art is effective in facilitating scientific innovations 中的 scientific innovations（科学创新）在原文（尤其是第 2、3 段例证链）没有对应；这个例子展示的是科学的传播，而非促成了什么新的科学发现或创新。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":33,"stem":"Some artists seem to worry about in the art-science partnership____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"their role may be underestimated."},{"key":"B","text":"their reputation may be impaired."},{"key":"C","text":"their creativity may be inhibited."},{"key":"D","text":"their work may be misguided."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"一些艺术家似乎担心在艺术与科学的合作中____","choicesZh":{"A":"他们的作用可能被低估。","B":"他们的声誉可能受损。","C":"他们的创造力可能受到抑制。","D":"他们的作品可能被误导。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干问的是艺术家\"担忧什么\"这一具体内容，是对原文一处明确表态的客观转述，不要求跨句推断。","parse":"题干为待补全陈述句，主干 Some artists seem to worry about____；seem to do 是\"似乎\"的弱化表态，worry about 后接宾语，由四个选项延续宾语；空格语义上等于 artists' worry。","locateEn":"But a genuine partnership must be a two-way street. Fewer artists than scientists responded to the Nature poll; however, several respondents noted that artists do not simply assist scientists with their communication requirements. Nor should their work be considered only as an object of study.","locateZh":"但真正的合作关系必须是双向的。回应《自然》民调的艺术家少于科学家；不过，一些受访者指出，艺术家不只是协助科学家完成传播任务。他们的作品也不应仅被当作研究对象。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是\"艺术家担心自身角色被矮化\"，their role may be underestimated 用 role + underestimated 精确对应两处并列否定的共同指向。","pairs":[{"orig":"artists do not simply assist scientists with their communication requirements","opt":"their role may be underestimated"},{"orig":"Nor should their work be considered only as an object of study","opt":"their role may be underestimated"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"their reputation may be impaired 中的 reputation（声誉）原文从未涉及——文中谈的是合作中的角色定位，没有讨论艺术家的名声是否受损。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"their creativity may be inhibited 中的 creativity（创造力）和 inhibited（被抑制）原文没有对应——艺术家在第 4 段表达的是\"角色被简化\"，并未说创作力被压制。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"their work may be misguided 中的 misguided（被误导/方向错误）原文不存在——原文说作品不应只被当作研究对象，没有说作品被错误引导。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":34,"stem":"What does the author say about CAYS?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It was headed alternately by artists and scientists."},{"key":"B","text":"It exemplified valuable art-science alliances."},{"key":"C","text":"Its projects aimed at advancing visual studies."},{"key":"D","text":"Its founders sought to raise the status of artists."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"作者对CAYS有何评价?","choicesZh":{"A":"它由艺术家和科学家交替领导。","B":"它体现了有价值的艺术与科学联盟。","C":"它的项目旨在推进视觉研究。","D":"它的创立者寻求提升艺术家的地位。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 What does the author say about CAYS? 是对作者就该机构所说内容的客观转述，属于典型细节题问法。","parse":"题干为疑问句，主干 What does the author say about CAYS?，What 作 say 的宾语，about CAYS 是状语指明话题；结构平铺、无陷阱。","locateEn":"More than half a century ago, the Massachusetts Institute of Technology opened its Center for Advanced Visual Studies (CAVS) to explore the role of technology in culture. The founders deliberately focused their projects around light—hence the \"visual studies\" in the name. Light was something that both artists and scientists had an interest in, and therefore could form the basis of collaboration.","locateZh":"半个多世纪前，麻省理工学院创办了\"先进视觉研究中心\"（CAVS），以探索技术在文化中的角色。创办者刻意把项目聚焦在\"光\"上——所以名字里有\"视觉研究\"。光是艺术家和科学家都感兴趣的事物，因此能成为他们合作的基础。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"It was headed alternately by artists and scientists 中的 headed alternately（轮流领导）原文没有任何依据——原文只说创办者刻意聚焦于光，未谈领导层如何轮换。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判要求选\"它是有价值的艺-科合作范例\"，It exemplified valuable art-science alliances 在 exemplify、valuable、art-science alliances 三个要素上严丝合缝。","pairs":[{"orig":"Light was something that both artists and scientists had an interest in, and therefore could form the basis of collaboration","opt":"It exemplified valuable art-science alliances"},{"orig":"looking to a time when leading researchers could also be artists, writers and poets, and vice versa","opt":"valuable art-science alliances"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Its projects aimed at advancing visual studies 把 CAVS 项目的目的偷换为\"推进视觉研究\"；原文明示项目是为了 explore the role of technology in culture，\"visual studies\" 只是因为聚焦于光而出现在名字里，并不是项目要推进的对象。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Its founders sought to raise the status of artists 中的 raise the status of artists（提高艺术家地位）原文没有依据——原文说创办者把项目聚焦在光上，未提及他们想抬高艺术家身份。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":35,"stem":"In the last paragraph, the author holds that art-science collaborations ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"are likely to go beyond public expectations."},{"key":"B","text":"will intensify interdisciplinary competition."},{"key":"C","text":"should do more than communicating science."},{"key":"D","text":"are becoming more popular than before."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"在最后一段中,作者认为艺术与科学的合作____","choicesZh":{"A":"很可能超出公众的期望。","B":"将加剧跨学科竞争。","C":"应该做的不仅仅是传播科学。","D":"正变得比以前更受欢迎。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 In the last paragraph, the author holds that art-science collaborations ____ 问作者在末段对话题持有的总观点，是末段层面的小主旨。","parse":"题干为待补全陈述句，主干 the author holds that art-science collaborations ____，that 引导宾语从句作 holds 的宾语，从句主语为 art-science collaborations，谓语由四个选项延续；In the last paragraph 是地点状语。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"are likely to go beyond public expectations 中的 public expectations（公众预期）原文没有——末段谈的是合作应超越\"科研传播\"功能，与公众预期无关；go beyond 一词制造了与正确项的伪同义陷阱。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"will intensify interdisciplinary competition 中 competition（竞争）与原文方向相反——原文末段主张的是双方合作加深、共同探索，而非把跨学科推向竞争。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判要求选\"应超出科研传播之外做更多\"，should do more than communicating science 在情态、超越关系、科学传播三要素上严格对应作者主张句。","pairs":[{"orig":"The reach of art-science tie-ups needs to go beyond the necessary purpose of research communication","opt":"should do more than communicating science"},{"orig":"participants must not fall into the trap of stereotyping each other","opt":"should do more than communicating science"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"are becoming more popular than before 只对应末段第一句 this trend is as strong as ever——但这是作者承认的事实背景，不是末段主张；题干问 author holds that，应取主张而非背景。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-4","kind":"reading","title":"Text 4","textNo":4,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 4","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-1","text":"The personal grievance provisions of New Zealand's Employment Relations Act 2000 (ERA) prevent an employer from firing an employee without good cause. Instead, dismissals must be justified. Employers must both show cause and act in a procedurally fair way."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-2","text":"Personal grievance procedures were designed to guard the jobs of ordinary workers from \"unjustified dismissals\". The premise was that the common law of contract lacked sufficient safeguards for workers against arbitrary conduct by management. Long gone are the days when a boss could simply give an employee contractual notice."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-3","text":"But these provisions create difficulties for businesses when applied to highly paid managers and executives. As countless boards and business owners will attest, constraining firms from firing poorly performing, high-earning managers is a handbrake on boosting productivity and overall performance. The difference between C-grade and A-grade managers may very well be the difference between business success or failure. Between preserving the jobs of ordinary workers or losing them. Yet mediocrity is no longer enough to justify a dismissal."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-4","text":"Consequently—and paradoxically—laws introduced to protect the jobs of ordinary workers may be placing those jobs at risk."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-5","text":"If not placing jobs at risk, to the extent employment protection laws constrain business owners from dismissing under-performing managers, those laws act as a constraint on firm productivity and therefore on workers' wages. Indeed, in \"An International Perspective on New Zealand's Productivity Paradox\" (2014), the Productivity Commission singled out the low quality of managerial capabilities as a cause of the country's poor productivity growth record."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-6","text":"Nor are highly paid managers themselves immune from the harm caused by the ERA'S unjustified dismissal procedures. Because employment protection laws make it costlier to fire an employee, employers are more cautious about hiring new staff. This makes it harder for the marginal manager to gain employment. And firms pay staff less because firms carry the burden of the employment arrangement going wrong."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-7","text":"Society also suffers from excessive employment protections. Stringent job dismissal regulations adversely affect productivity growth and hamper both prosperity and overall well-being."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-8","text":"Across the Tasman Sea, Australia deals with the unjustified dismissal paradox by excluding employees earning above a specified \"high-income threshold\" from the protection of its unfair dismissal laws. In New Zealand, a 2016 private members' Bill tried to permit firms and high-income employees to contract out of the unjustified dismissal regime. However, the mechanisms proposed were unwieldy and the Bill was voted down following the change in government later that year."}]},"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"The personal grievance provisions of the ERA are intended to____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"punish dubious corporate practices."},{"key":"B","text":"improve traditional hiring procedures."},{"key":"C","text":"exempt employers from certain duties."},{"key":"D","text":"protect the rights of ordinary workers."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"ERA的个人申诉条款旨在","choicesZh":{"A":"惩罚可疑的企业行为。","B":"改进传统的招聘程序。","C":"使雇主免除某些义务。","D":"保护普通劳动者的权利。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The personal grievance provisions of the ERA are intended to____ 问的是「被设计来做什么」这一客观事实，没有 infer/imply 字样。","parse":"The personal grievance provisions of the ERA are intended to____ 主干是 provisions are intended to do sth，被动语态＋不定式，选项就是这个不定式的内容。结构无陷阱。","locateEn":"Personal grievance procedures were designed to guard the jobs of ordinary workers from \"unjustified dismissals\".","locateZh":"人事申诉程序的设立目的，是保护普通工人的岗位免遭「不公正解雇」。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"punish dubious corporate practices 谈「惩罚可疑企业行为」，但原文只说该条款 prevent…firing without good cause，是约束而非惩罚，且对象是 dismissal 行为而非泛 corporate practices。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"improve traditional hiring procedures 谈「改良招聘流程」，但 dismissals must be justified 与 guard the jobs of ordinary workers from \"unjustified dismissals\" 全部围绕解雇，不涉及招聘。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"exempt employers from certain duties 是「免除雇主义务」，但条款做的恰恰相反——给雇主加上 show cause 与 procedurally fair 两道义务（Employers must both show cause and act in a procedurally fair way）。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是「保护普通工人」，D 项 protect the rights of ordinary workers 在受益对象与立法目的两点上都与定位句对应，是唯一在两点上全等的选项。","pairs":[{"orig":"guard the jobs of ordinary workers","opt":"protect the rights of ordinary workers"},{"orig":"ordinary workers","opt":"ordinary workers"}]}}}}},{"n":37,"stem":"It can be learned from Paragraph 3 that the provisions may____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"hinder business development."},{"key":"B","text":"undermine managers' authority."},{"key":"C","text":"affect the public image of the firms."},{"key":"D","text":"worsen labor-management relations."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"推断题","stemZh":"从第3段可以了解到,这些条款可能会","choicesZh":{"A":"阻碍企业发展。","B":"削弱管理者的权威。","C":"影响公司的公众形象。","D":"恶化劳资关系。"},"analysis":{"type":"It can be learned from Paragraph 3 that the provisions may____ 中 can be learned 与 may 都是推断信号，要求从段内事实做一步合理延伸。","parse":"It can be learned from Paragraph 3 that the provisions may____ 是 It + 被动 + that 从句结构，that 从句的主语是 the provisions，选项是 may 后面的动词短语；句式平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"constraining firms from firing poorly performing, high-earning managers is a handbrake on boosting productivity and overall performance.","locateZh":"约束企业解雇业绩差、薪资高的管理者，是阻碍生产率与整体绩效提升的手刹。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是「拖累企业」，A 项正是把 handbrake 比喻去掉、把 productivity 上位为 business development 的标准推断题答案。","pairs":[{"orig":"a handbrake on boosting productivity and overall performance","opt":"hinder business development"},{"orig":"create difficulties for businesses","opt":"hinder business development"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"undermine managers' authority 谈「削弱管理者权威」，但本段说的是「无法解雇差管理者」，受影响的是企业／老板的解雇权，不是管理者自己的权威。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"affect the public image of the firms 谈「影响企业公众形象」，但第 3 段只谈生产率、绩效、业务成败，未涉及「公众形象」维度。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"worsen labor-management relations 谈「恶化劳资关系」，但第 3 段对比的是「差管理者 vs 好管理者」和「普通工人岗位保留 vs 丢失」，并未涉及劳资双方对立关系。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":38,"stem":"Which of the following measures would the Productivity Commission support?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Imposing reasonable wage restraints."},{"key":"B","text":"Enforcing employment protection laws."},{"key":"C","text":"Limiting the powers of business owners."},{"key":"D","text":"Dismissing poorly performing managers."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"推断题","stemZh":"生产力委员会会支持以下哪项措施?","choicesZh":{"A":"实施合理的工资限制。","B":"执行就业保护法律。","C":"限制企业所有者的权力。","D":"解雇表现不佳的管理者。"},"analysis":{"type":"Which of the following measures would the Productivity Commission support? 原文只点出该机构对生产率低的归因，「会支持哪条措施」需要由归因反推一步。","parse":"Which of the following measures would the Productivity Commission support? 是 Which + 名词 + would + 主语 + 动词 的虚拟语气问句；would 提示推断、support 是立场动词，结构简洁无陷阱。","locateEn":"the Productivity Commission singled out the low quality of managerial capabilities as a cause of the country's poor productivity growth record.","locateZh":"生产率委员会单独点名「管理能力低下」是新西兰生产率增长记录差的一大成因。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Imposing reasonable wage restraints 谈「合理薪酬管制」，但 P5 委员会的归因落在「管理能力」而非「管理者薪酬」上，没有任何句子支持工资管制立场。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"Enforcing employment protection laws 谈「加强用工保护法」，但全文（含 P5）的论点恰恰是这类法律 act as a constraint on firm productivity，委员会立场必然与「加强它」相反。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Limiting the powers of business owners 谈「限制企业主权力」，但全文都在说应给企业主松绑（解雇差管理者的权），委员会自然不会支持反向限权。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是「换掉差管理者」，D 项 Dismissing poorly performing managers 是把诊断结论变成对症措施的唯一选项。","pairs":[{"orig":"the low quality of managerial capabilities","opt":"Dismissing poorly performing managers"},{"orig":"as a cause of the country's poor productivity growth record","opt":"Dismissing poorly performing managers"}]}}}}},{"n":39,"stem":"What might be an effect of ERA's unjustified dismissal procedures?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Highly paid managers lose their jobs."},{"key":"B","text":"Employees suffer from salary cuts."},{"key":"C","text":"Society sees a rise in overall well-being."},{"key":"D","text":"Employers need to hire new staff."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"ERA的不当解雇程序可能会产生什么影响?","choicesZh":{"A":"高薪管理者失去工作。","B":"员工遭受薪资削减。","C":"社会整体福祉上升。","D":"雇主需要雇用新员工。"},"analysis":{"type":"What might be an effect of ERA's unjustified dismissal procedures? 问的是已被原文明说的后果之一，might 是事实层面的「可能列举之一」，不是推断信号。","parse":"What might be an effect of ERA's unjustified dismissal procedures? 主干是 What might be an effect，of 短语限定 effect 的归属对象；句式直白，无陷阱。","locateEn":"If not placing jobs at risk, to the extent employment protection laws constrain business owners from dismissing under-performing managers, those laws act as a constraint on firm productivity and therefore on workers' wages.","locateZh":"即便不至于把岗位置于风险，只要用工保护法约束了企业主解雇业绩差的管理者，这些法律就成了企业生产率的束缚，进而压低了工人的工资。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Highly paid managers lose their jobs 谈「高薪管理者丢工作」，但原文恰恰说这类条款让差管理者难以被解雇 constraining firms from firing poorly performing, high-earning managers，方向相反。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是「工资被压低」，B 项 Employees suffer from salary cuts 在 P5 与 P6 都有直接原文支撑，是唯一一项被原文两次明示的后果。","pairs":[{"orig":"therefore on workers' wages","opt":"Employees suffer from salary cuts"},{"orig":"firms pay staff less","opt":"Employees suffer from salary cuts"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Society sees a rise in overall well-being 谈「社会整体福祉上升」，但 Stringent job dismissal regulations adversely affect productivity growth and hamper both prosperity and overall well-being 明说是 hamper（损害），方向相反。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Employers need to hire new staff 谈「雇主需要招新人」，但 employers are more cautious about hiring new staff 说的恰是雇主更不愿招人，方向相反。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":40,"stem":"It can be inferred that the \"high-income threshold\" in Australia____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"has secured managers' earnings."},{"key":"B","text":"has produced undesired results."},{"key":"C","text":"is beneficial to business owners."},{"key":"D","text":"is difficult to put into practice"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"推断题","stemZh":"可以推断出,澳大利亚的\"高收入门槛\"","choicesZh":{"A":"保障了管理者的收入。","B":"产生了不良后果。","C":"对企业所有者有利。","D":"难以付诸实践"},"analysis":{"type":"It can be inferred that the \"high-income threshold\" in Australia____ 直接用 inferred 标明本题需从原文做一步合理推断。","parse":"It can be inferred that the \"high-income threshold\" in Australia____ 是 It + 被动 + that 从句，主语是带引号的术语，谓语和选项相连构成完整陈述；引号只是术语标记，不是修辞，不必过度解读。","locateEn":"Australia deals with the unjustified dismissal paradox by excluding employees earning above a specified \"high-income threshold\" from the protection of its unfair dismissal laws.","locateZh":"澳大利亚处理这一不公正解雇悖论的办法，是把收入高于特定「高收入门槛」的雇员排除在不公平解雇法的保护范围之外。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"has secured managers' earnings 谈「保住了管理者的收入」，但 P8 只说门槛划定了保护边界，未谈管理者收入是否被保障；况且该门槛针对的是雇佣保护，不针对薪酬本身。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"has produced undesired results 谈「产生了不良后果」，但 P8 对澳大利亚做法是中性陈述其机制；唯一被作者评价为「不良」的是新西兰 2016 年那次失败的 Bill（the mechanisms proposed were unwieldy）。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是「利好企业主」，C 项是唯一同时被定位句机制和全文论点链双重支撑的选项。","pairs":[{"orig":"excluding employees earning above a specified \"high-income threshold\" from the protection of its unfair dismissal laws","opt":"is beneficial to business owners"},{"orig":"constraining firms from firing poorly performing, high-earning managers is a handbrake on boosting productivity and overall performance","opt":"is beneficial to business owners"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"is difficult to put into practice 谈「难以落地」，但澳大利亚的门槛是已经在用的现行做法（Australia deals with the unjustified dismissal paradox by excluding 用一般现在时陈述），无落地难的暗示；新西兰版本才被评为 unwieldy。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-b","kind":"matching","title":"Section II Part B 选句填空","directions":"Directions: In the following text, there are five people’s opinions about Emma Manis’s article “ The Case Against Zoos”. For Questions 41-45, choose the best statement from the list A-G to summarize each numbered person’s opinion. There are two extra choices which do not fit in any of the blanks. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-b-1","label":"","text":"Teri Byrd: {{41}}"},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-2","label":"","text":"I was a zoo and wildlife park employee for years. Both the wildlife park and zoo claimed to be operating for the benefit of the animals and for conservation purposes. This claim was false. Neither one of them actually participated in any contributions to animal research or conservation. They are profitable institutions whose bottom line is much more important than the condition of the animals."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-3","label":"","text":"Animals despise being captives in zoos. No matter how you \"enhance\" enclosures, they do not allow for freedom, a natural diet or adequate exercise. Animals end up stressed and unhealthy or dead. It's past time for transparency with these institutions, and it's past time to eliminate zoos from our culture."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-4","label":"","text":"Karen R. Sime: {{42}}"},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-5","label":"","text":"As a zoology professor, I agree with Emma Marris that zoo displays can be sad and cruel. But she underestimates the educational value of zoos."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-6","label":"","text":"The zoology program at my university attracts students for whom zoo visits were the crucial formative experience that led them to major in biological sciences. These are mostly students who had no opportunity as children to travel to wilderness areas, wildlife refuges or national parks. Although good TV shows can help stir children's interest in conservation, they cannot replace the excitement of a zoo visit as an intense, immersive and interactive experience. Surely there must be some middle ground that balances zoos' treatment of animals with their educational potential."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-7","label":"","text":"Greg Newberry: {{43}}"},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-8","label":"","text":"Emma Marris's article is an insult and a disservice to the thousands of passionate people who work tirelessly to improve the lives of animals and protect our planet. She uses outdated research and decades-old examples to undermine the noble mission of organizations committed to connecting children to a world beyond their own."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-9","label":"","text":"Zoos are at the forefront of conservation and constantly evolving to improve how they care for animals and protect each species in its natural habitat. Are there tragedies? Of course. But they are the exception, not the norm that Ms. Marris implies. A distressed animal in a zoo will get as good or better treatment than most of us at our local hospital."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-10","label":"","text":"Dean Gallea: {{44}}"},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-11","label":"","text":"As a fellow environmentalist, animal-protection advocate and longtime vegetarian, I could properly be in the same camp as Emma Marris on the issue of zoos. But I believe that well-run zoos, and the heroic animals that suffer their captivity, do serve a higher purpose. Were it not for opportunities to observe these beautiful, wild creatures close to home, many more people would be driven by their fascination to travel to wild areas to seek out, disturb and even hunt them down."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-12","label":"","text":"Zoos are, in that sense, similar to natural history and archeology museums, serving to satisfy our need for contact with these living creatures while leaving the vast majority undisturbed in their natural environments."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-13","label":"","text":"John Fraser: {{45}}"},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-14","label":"","text":"Emma Marris selectively describes and misrepresents the findings of our research. Our studies focused on the impact of zoo experiences on how people think about themselves and nature, and the data points extracted from our studies do not, in any way, discount what is learned in a zoo visit."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-15","label":"","text":"Zoos are tools for thinking. Our research provides strong support for the value of zoos in connecting people with animals and with nature. Zoos provide a critical voice for conservation and environmental protection. They afford an opportunity for people from all backgrounds to encounter a range of animals, from drone bees to springbok or salmon, to better understand the natural world we live in."}]},"choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Zoos which spare no effort to take care of animals should not be subjected to unfair criticism."},{"key":"B","text":"To pressure zoos to spend less on their animals would lead to inhumane outcomes for the precious creatures in their care."},{"key":"C","text":"While animals in captivity deserve sympathy, zoos play a significant role in starting young people down the path of related sciences."},{"key":"D","text":"Zoos save people trips to wilderness areas and thus contribute to wildlife conservation."},{"key":"E","text":"For wild animals that cannot be returned to their natural habitats, zoos offer the best alternative."},{"key":"F","text":"Zoos should have been closed down as they prioritize money making over animals' wellbeing."},{"key":"G","text":"Marris distorts our findings which actually prove that zoos serve as an indispensable link between man and nature."}],"questions":[{"n":41,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Zoos which spare no effort to take care of animals should not be subjected to unfair criticism."},{"key":"B","text":"To pressure zoos to spend less on their animals would lead to inhumane outcomes for the precious creatures in their care."},{"key":"C","text":"While animals in captivity deserve sympathy, zoos play a significant role in starting young people down the path of related sciences."},{"key":"D","text":"Zoos save people trips to wilderness areas and thus contribute to wildlife conservation."},{"key":"E","text":"For wild animals that cannot be returned to their natural habitats, zoos offer the best alternative."},{"key":"F","text":"Zoos should have been closed down as they prioritize money making over animals' wellbeing."},{"key":"G","text":"Marris distorts our findings which actually prove that zoos serve as an indispensable link between man and nature."}],"answer":"F","explanation":{"answer":"F","type":"段尾","stemZh":"41. Teri Byrd: (41)____","reason":"Zoos should have been closed down as they prioritize money making over animals' wellbeing. 动物园本应关闭,因为它们将赚钱置于动物福祉之上。\n动物园是逐利伪善、应予取缔（强烈反对） Teri Byrd 以前雇员身份揭露：动物园号称为动物和保育，This claim was false（这说法是假的），它们是 profitable institutions whose bottom line is much more important than the condition of the animals（利润机构，盈亏底线远比动物处境重要）；圈养让动物 stressed and unhealthy or dead。结尾给出最明确的立场句 it's past time to eliminate zoos from our culture（早就该把动物园从我们的文化中清除）。立场是坚决反对、主张取缔，理由是\"逐利压倒动物福祉\"。这是她本人的主张，不是转述他人。\n同义对上：关闭动物园 + 逐利压过动物福祉 F 与全段主旨逐点同义：Zoos should have been closed down（本应关闭）对应 Byrd 的 it's past time to eliminate zoos；F 的 they prioritize money making over animals' wellbeing（把赚钱看得比动物福祉重）对应原文 bottom line is much more important than the condition of the animals。两条核心——\"关闭\"与\"逐利伤动物\"——都对上，是整段立场的同义改写。","options":{},"choiceEn":"Zoos should have been closed down as they prioritize money making over animals' wellbeing.","clues":[]}},{"n":42,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Zoos which spare no effort to take care of animals should not be subjected to unfair criticism."},{"key":"B","text":"To pressure zoos to spend less on their animals would lead to inhumane outcomes for the precious creatures in their care."},{"key":"C","text":"While animals in captivity deserve sympathy, zoos play a significant role in starting young people down the path of related sciences."},{"key":"D","text":"Zoos save people trips to wilderness areas and thus contribute to wildlife conservation."},{"key":"E","text":"For wild animals that cannot be returned to their natural habitats, zoos offer the best alternative."},{"key":"F","text":"Zoos should have been closed down as they prioritize money making over animals' wellbeing."},{"key":"G","text":"Marris distorts our findings which actually prove that zoos serve as an indispensable link between man and nature."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"强转折后","stemZh":"42. Karen R. Sime: (42)____","reason":"While animals in captivity deserve sympathy, zoos play a significant role in starting young people down the path of related sciences. 尽管圈养动物值得同情,但动物园在引导年轻人走上相关科学道路方面发挥着重要作用。\n承认展览残忍，但重在动物园的教育价值（折中） Sime 以动物学教授身份先让步：I agree with Emma Marris that zoo displays can be sad and cruel（同意展览可悲又残忍），随即用 But 转折点出真正立场 But she underestimates the educational value of zoos（但她低估了动物园的教育价值）。下段举学生因参观动物园而选生物学专业为例，归到\"动物园有教育/启蒙价值\"这句观点上，并提出 there must be some middle ground（必有折中之地）。立场是折中：既同情动物、又力挺教育功能。转折后那句才是她的主张，不是她引述的 Marris 观点。\n同义对上：同情圈养动物 + 引年轻人入科学 C 的让步段 While animals in captivity deserve sympathy（圈养动物值得同情）同义对应 zoo displays can be sad and cruel；C 的主句 zoos play a significant role in starting young people down the path of related sciences（动物园在引年轻人走上相关科学之路上作用重大）正是 the educational value of zoos 的具体化，呼应 led them to major in biological sciences。让步+肯定的双层结构与全段完全同构。","options":{},"choiceEn":"While animals in captivity deserve sympathy, zoos play a significant role in starting young people down the path of related sciences.","clues":[]}},{"n":43,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Zoos which spare no effort to take care of animals should not be subjected to unfair criticism."},{"key":"B","text":"To pressure zoos to spend less on their animals would lead to inhumane outcomes for the precious creatures in their care."},{"key":"C","text":"While animals in captivity deserve sympathy, zoos play a significant role in starting young people down the path of related sciences."},{"key":"D","text":"Zoos save people trips to wilderness areas and thus contribute to wildlife conservation."},{"key":"E","text":"For wild animals that cannot be returned to their natural habitats, zoos offer the best alternative."},{"key":"F","text":"Zoos should have been closed down as they prioritize money making over animals' wellbeing."},{"key":"G","text":"Marris distorts our findings which actually prove that zoos serve as an indispensable link between man and nature."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"通篇归纳","stemZh":"43. Greg Newberry: (43)____","reason":"Zoos which spare no effort to take care of animals should not be subjected to unfair criticism. 不遗余力照顾动物的动物园不应受到不公正的批评。\n动物园在保育前沿，Marris 的批评是侮辱、不公（强辩护） Newberry 火力全开维护动物园：称文章 an insult and a disservice（侮辱与伤害），指 Marris uses outdated research and decades-old examples（用过时研究和几十年前的旧例）来抹黑高尚使命。又说动物园 at the forefront of conservation（处于保育前沿），承认有悲剧但 they are the exception, not the norm（是例外而非常态）。立场是强烈支持，核心情绪是\"动物园被不公平地批评了\"。\n同义对上：尽力照料的动物园不该被不公批评 A 的 Zoos which spare no effort to take care of animals（竭力照料动物的动物园）对应 constantly evolving to improve how they care for animals、get as good or better treatment than most of us at our local hospital；A 的 should not be subjected to unfair criticism（不该受不公批评）正是 Newberry 全段的核心情绪——他把 Marris 的文章定性为 an insult 的不实之词。\"尽力照料\"+\"反对不公批评\"两点同义对上。","options":{},"choiceEn":"Zoos which spare no effort to take care of animals should not be subjected to unfair criticism.","clues":[]}},{"n":44,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Zoos which spare no effort to take care of animals should not be subjected to unfair criticism."},{"key":"B","text":"To pressure zoos to spend less on their animals would lead to inhumane outcomes for the precious creatures in their care."},{"key":"C","text":"While animals in captivity deserve sympathy, zoos play a significant role in starting young people down the path of related sciences."},{"key":"D","text":"Zoos save people trips to wilderness areas and thus contribute to wildlife conservation."},{"key":"E","text":"For wild animals that cannot be returned to their natural habitats, zoos offer the best alternative."},{"key":"F","text":"Zoos should have been closed down as they prioritize money making over animals' wellbeing."},{"key":"G","text":"Marris distorts our findings which actually prove that zoos serve as an indispensable link between man and nature."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"强转折后","stemZh":"44. Dean Gallea: (44)____","reason":"Zoos save people trips to wilderness areas and thus contribute to wildlife conservation. 动物园使人们免于前往荒野地区,从而有助于野生动物保护。\n好动物园让人们免去野外打扰，从而保护野生动物（支持） Gallea 先自陈本可与 Marris 同阵营（I could properly be in the same camp as Emma Marris），随即 But 转折表态 well-run zoos, and the heroic animals that suffer their captivity, do serve a higher purpose（运营良好的动物园确有更高目的）。理由是：若没有在家门口观赏野生动物的机会，many more people would be driven by their fascination to travel to wild areas to seek out, disturb and even hunt them down（更多人会跑去野外搜寻、打扰甚至猎杀它们）；动物园像博物馆一样 leaving the vast majority undisturbed in their natural environments（让绝大多数动物在自然中不被打扰）。立场是支持，独特理由是\"省去野外打扰=保护野生动物\"。But 后才是他本人主张，前面的\"同阵营\"是欲扬先抑。\n同义对上：省去野外之行→助力野生动物保护 D 的 save people trips to wilderness areas（省去人们去荒野的旅行）精确对应 many more people would be driven by their fascination to travel to wild areas to seek out, disturb and even hunt them down 的反面——有了动物园人们就不必/不会去野外打扰；D 的 contribute to wildlife conservation（从而有助保护野生动物）对应 leaving the vast majority undisturbed in their natural environments。这条独特因果链与 D 一一吻合。","options":{},"choiceEn":"Zoos save people trips to wilderness areas and thus contribute to wildlife conservation.","clues":[]}},{"n":45,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Zoos which spare no effort to take care of animals should not be subjected to unfair criticism."},{"key":"B","text":"To pressure zoos to spend less on their animals would lead to inhumane outcomes for the precious creatures in their care."},{"key":"C","text":"While animals in captivity deserve sympathy, zoos play a significant role in starting young people down the path of related sciences."},{"key":"D","text":"Zoos save people trips to wilderness areas and thus contribute to wildlife conservation."},{"key":"E","text":"For wild animals that cannot be returned to their natural habitats, zoos offer the best alternative."},{"key":"F","text":"Zoos should have been closed down as they prioritize money making over animals' wellbeing."},{"key":"G","text":"Marris distorts our findings which actually prove that zoos serve as an indispensable link between man and nature."}],"answer":"G","explanation":{"answer":"G","type":"段首","stemZh":"45. John Fraser: (45)____","reason":"Marris distorts our findings which actually prove that zoos serve as an indispensable link between man and nature. Marris歪曲了我们的研究成果,而这些成果实际上证明了动物园是人与自然之间不可或缺的纽带。\nMarris 歪曲了我们的研究，研究其实证明动物园价值（支持+维权） Fraser 是被引研究的作者本人，开篇即定调 Emma Marris selectively describes and misrepresents the findings of our research（Marris 选择性描述并歪曲了我们研究的发现），强调数据 do not, in any way, discount what is learned in a zoo visit（绝不贬低参观动物园所学）。下段正面立论 Our research provides strong support for the value of zoos in connecting people with animals and with nature（我们的研究有力支持动物园在连接人与动物、人与自然上的价值）。立场是支持，独特身份是\"研究被歪曲的当事人\"，核心是\"为自己的研究正名\"。\n同义对上：Marris 歪曲我们研究，研究证动物园是人与自然纽带 G 几乎逐点复刻：Marris distorts our findings（Marris 歪曲我们的研究发现）对应 misrepresents the findings of our research；G 的 zoos serve as an indispensable link between man and nature（动物园是人与自然不可缺的纽带）对应 the value of zoos in connecting people with animals and with nature。\"歪曲我们研究\"+\"动物园连接人与自然\"两点同义对上，连第一人称 our 都一致。","options":{},"choiceEn":"Marris distorts our findings which actually prove that zoos serve as an indispensable link between man and nature.","clues":[]}}]},{"id":"section2-part-c","kind":"translation","title":"Section II Part C 英译汉","directions":"Directions: Read the following text carefully and then translate the underlined segments into Chinese. Write your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. ( 10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-c-1","text":"Between 1807 and 1814 the Iberian Peninsula (comprising Spain and Portugal) was the scene of a titanic and merciless struggle. It took place on many different planes: between Napoleon's French army and the angry inhabitants; between the British, ever keen to exacerbate the emperor's difficulties, and the marshals sent from Paris to try to keep them in check; between new forces of science and meritocracy and old ones of conservatism and birth. (46) It was also, and this is unknown even to many people well read about the period, a battle between those who made codes and those who broke them."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-2","text":"I first discovered the Napoleonic cryptographic battle a few years ago when I was reading Sir Charles Oman's epic History of the Peninsular War. In volume V he had attached an appendix, The Scovell Ciphers. (47) It listed many documents in code that had been captured from the French army of Spain, and whose secrets had been revealed by the work of one George Scovell, an officer in British headquarters. Oman rated Scovell's significance highly, but at the same time, the general nature of his History meant that (48) he could not analyze carefully what this obscure officer may or may not have contributed to that great struggle between nations or indeed tell us anything much about the man himself. I was keen to read more, but was surprised to find that Oman's appendix, published in 1914, was the only considered thing that had been written about this secret war."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-3","text":"I became convinced that this story was every bit as exciting and significant as that of Enigma and the breaking of German codes in the Second World War. The question was, could it be told?"},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-4","text":"Studying Scovell's papers at the Public Record Office, London, I found that he had left an extensive journal and copious notes about his work in the peninsula. What was more, many original French dispatches had been preserved in this collection, which I realized was priceless. (49) There may have been many spies and intelligence officers during the Napoleonic Wars, but it is usually extremely difficult to find the material they actually provided or worked on."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-5","text":"As I researched Scovell's story I found far more of interest besides his intelligence work. His status in Lord Wellington's headquarters and the recognition given to him for his work were all bound up with the class politics of the army at the time. His tale of self-improvement and hard work would make a fascinating biography in its own right, but represents something more than that. (50) Just as the code breaking has its wider relevance in the struggle for Spain, so his attempts to make his way up the promotion ladder speak volumes about British society."}]},"sentences":[{"n":46,"en":"It was also, and this is unknown even to many people well read about the period, a battle between those who made codes and those who broke them.","refZh":"这场较量同样还是一场编制密码者与破译密码者之间的较量——而这一点，甚至连许多熟知那段历史的人也并不知晓。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"It was also,","lit":"它(这场较量)也是","role":"（主干主谓(系表，宾语被插入语隔开)）","note":"难点： It 回指还原 + also 承接上文 It 不要译成空泛的「它」。回到本段 the Iberian Peninsula ... was the scene of a titanic and merciless struggle，It 指「这场较量/这场斗争」；also 承接前文连续几组 between... 并列，意为「(这场较量)同样还是一场……」。"},{"en":"and this is unknown even to many people well read about the period,","lit":"——而这一点甚至连许多熟读这段历史的人也并不知晓——","role":"（插入语(and 引导的补充说明)）","note":"难点： well read 熟词僻义 + 插入语要归位 well read 不是「读得好」，而是固定搭配「博览群书的、熟知某领域的」，well read about the period = 熟知这段历史的(人)。这一整段被两个逗号夹在主干中间，是插入语，翻译时要把它从主干里拎出来、单独处理(用破折号或单独成句)，不能顺着英文语序硬塞进主谓之间。"},{"en":"a battle between those who made codes and those who broke them.","lit":"一场编制密码的人与破译密码的人之间的较量","role":"（表语(a battle + 两个 who 定语从句)）","note":"难点： those who... 译「……的人」+ them 指代 codes those who made codes/those who broke them 是两个 who 定语从句，译法固定为「编制密码的(那些)人」「破译密码的(那些)人」。末尾 them 回指 codes，broke them = 破译密码；结合 those who made codes and those who broke them 全篇术语统一作「密码」。"}]},{"n":47,"en":"It listed many documents in code that had been captured from the French army of Spain, and whose secrets had been revealed by the work of one George Scovell, an officer in British headquarters.","refZh":"这部附录列出了许多用密码写成的文件，这些文件是从驻西班牙的法军那里缴获的，而其中的秘密则是由一位名叫乔治·斯科维尔的人破解的——他是英军总部的一名军官。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"It listed many documents in code","lit":"它(这部附录)列出了许多用密码写成的文件","role":"（主干主谓宾(in code 作 documents 的后置定语)）","note":"难点： It 回指附录 + in code 译「用密码写的」 It 回指上一句的 an appendix, The Scovell Ciphers，即《斯科维尔密码》那部附录。documents in code 不是「密码里的文件」，in code 是后置定语「以密码形式的、用密码写成的」，整体译「用密码写成的文件/密码文件」。"},{"en":"that had been captured from the French army of Spain,","lit":"这些文件是从西班牙的法国军队那里缴获的","role":"（that 定语从句(修饰 documents，被动语态)）","note":"难点： 被动 had been captured 转主动/无主句 + the French army of Spain had been captured 是被动，汉语少用「被」，可转译为「(这些文件是)从……缴获来的」。the French army of Spain 指「驻西班牙的法军/在西班牙作战的法军」，对应 the French army of Spain，不是「西班牙的法国军队」这种生硬直译。"},{"en":"and whose secrets had been revealed by the work of one George Scovell,","lit":"而这些文件的秘密是由一位名叫乔治·斯科维尔的人的工作揭开的","role":"（whose 定语从句(并列第二个定语，修饰 documents，被动)）","note":"难点： whose=these documents' + 被动转主动 + one 表「一位」 whose 指 documents 的所属，whose secrets = 这些(密码)文件的秘密。had been revealed by 被动转主动：把 by 后的施事 the work of one George Scovell 提为主语——「乔治·斯科维尔的工作揭开了这些文件的秘密」。one George Scovell 中 one 不是数字「一个」，而是引出人名的「某位、一位名叫……的」。"},{"en":"an officer in British headquarters.","lit":"他是英军总部的一名军官","role":"（同位语(说明 George Scovell 身份)）","note":"难点： 同位语补出「他是」 这是 George Scovell 的同位语，汉译时补一个「他是」或「即」，译成「(他是)英军总部的一名军官」，让句子顺。"}]},{"n":48,"en":"he could not analyze carefully what this obscure officer may or may not have contributed to that great struggle between nations or indeed tell us anything much about the man himself.","refZh":"他无法仔细分析这位默默无闻的军官对那场民族间的大较量究竟做出过怎样的贡献、或是否做出过贡献，甚至也无法就此人其人本身告诉我们多少情况。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"he could not analyze carefully","lit":"他(Oman)无法仔细分析","role":"（主干主谓(情态动词否定)）","note":"难点： he 回指 Oman + could not 否定管到句末 he 指上文的史学家 Oman(见 Oman rated Scovell's significance highly)，不是 Scovell。could not 的否定一直管到后面的 or ... tell us 这个并列项，即「既不能分析……，也无法告诉我们……」。"},{"en":"what this obscure officer may or may not have contributed to that great struggle between nations","lit":"这位不知名的军官对那场国家间的大较量到底贡献了什么、或没有贡献什么","role":"（what 引导的宾语从句(作 analyze 的宾语)）","note":"难点： this obscure officer 指 Scovell + may or may not 译「是否/有没有」 this obscure officer(这位默默无闻/不知名的军官)指 Scovell。may or may not have contributed 是「可能贡献过、也可能没贡献过」，汉语顺译为「到底有没有(做出)贡献/贡献了什么或没贡献什么」。that great struggle between nations 回指 a titanic and merciless struggle，即那场国家/民族之间的大较量。"},{"en":"or indeed tell us anything much about the man himself.","lit":"或者说，也无法告诉我们关于这个人本身的任何多少情况","role":"（并列谓语(与 analyze 并列，共用 could not)）","note":"难点： or 并列受 could not 统辖 + anything much 译「多少」 or indeed tell us 与前面 analyze 并列，同受 could not 否定，意为「甚至也无法告诉我们……」。否定 + anything much 表「几乎说不出什么、谈不上多少」。the man himself = 这个人本身/此人其人，指 Scovell。"}]},{"n":49,"en":"There may have been many spies and intelligence officers during the Napoleonic Wars, but it is usually extremely difficult to find the material they actually provided or worked on.","refZh":"拿破仑战争期间或许曾有过许多间谍和情报人员，但通常极难找到他们实际提供过或经手过的那些材料。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"There may have been many spies and intelligence officers during the Napoleonic Wars,","lit":"在拿破仑战争期间，可能曾有过许多间谍和情报军官","role":"（前一分句(there be 句型 + 时间状语)）","note":"难点： may have been 表对过去的推测「可能曾经有过」 may have been 是「情态动词 + have done」，表示对过去的推测，译「可能(曾经)有过」，不能丢掉这层不确定语气。intelligence officers = 情报军官/情报人员，intelligence 此处取「情报」义，不是「智力」。"},{"en":"but it is usually extremely difficult to find the material","lit":"但通常极难找到这些材料","role":"（后一分句主干(it 形式主语，真主语为 to find...)）","note":"难点： it 是形式主语，真主语是后面的 to find... it is difficult to find... 中 it 不指代任何具体事物，是形式主语，真正的主语是不定式 to find the material。译时直接译「(要)找到……是极其困难的」或「通常极难找到……」，绝不能把 it 译成「它」。"},{"en":"they actually provided or worked on.","lit":"他们实际提供过或经手过的(材料)","role":"（定语从句(省略 that，修饰 the material)）","note":"难点： 省略关系词的定语从句 + they 回指 spies and intelligence officers the material 后省略了 that/which，they actually provided or worked on 是定语从句修饰 the material。they 回指前一分句的 many spies and intelligence officers。worked on 是「从事、经手、处理」，与 provided「提供」并列，译「他们实际提供或经手过的材料」。"}]},{"n":50,"en":"Just as the code breaking has its wider relevance in the struggle for Spain, so his attempts to make his way up the promotion ladder speak volumes about British society.","refZh":"正如密码破译在那场争夺西班牙的较量中有着更为广泛的意义，他力争沿着晋升阶梯往上爬的种种努力，也同样很能说明当时英国社会的状况。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"Just as the code breaking has its wider relevance in the struggle for Spain,","lit":"正如密码破译在争夺西班牙的斗争中有其更广泛的意义","role":"（Just as 引导的方式/类比状语从句）","note":"难点： Just as... so... 类比结构 + wider relevance 译「更广泛的意义」 Just as A, so B 是固定的类比结构，译「正如A……，B也(同样)……」。the code breaking 承接全篇主题，译「密码破译」(与 46/47 术语一致)。wider relevance 不是「更宽的关联」，relevance 此处取「意义、关联性、重要性」，wider relevance = 更广泛的意义/更深远的关联。the struggle for Spain = 争夺西班牙的斗争/那场争夺西班牙的较量。"},{"en":"so his attempts to make his way up the promotion ladder","lit":"他努力沿着晋升的阶梯往上爬的种种尝试","role":"（主句主语(so 呼应 Just as，his attempts 为主语，to 不定式作定语)）","note":"难点： make his way up the promotion ladder 比喻义「设法晋升/往上爬」 make one's way up the promotion ladder 是比喻：the promotion ladder「晋升的阶梯/官阶」，make his way up 是「努力往上爬/谋求晋升」。整体 his attempts to... = 他谋求晋升/力争上位的种种努力。his 指上文的 Scovell。"},{"en":"speak volumes about British society.","lit":"极能说明英国社会的状况","role":"（主句谓语(主句谓语动词 + 宾语)）","note":"难点： speak volumes 习语「很说明问题/意味深长」 speak volumes 是固定习语，字面「说了好几大卷」，实义「(无须多言就)很能说明问题、极具说明意义」。speak volumes about British society = 很能说明/充分反映当时英国社会的状况。绝不能直译成「讲了关于英国社会的好多卷书」。"}]}]},{"id":"section3-part-a","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part A 写作","directions":"51. Directions: Write an email to a professor at a British university, inviting him/her to organize a team for the international innovation contest to be held at your university. You should write about 100 words on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not use your own name. Use “Li Ming” instead. (10 points)","prompt":"51. Directions: Write an email to a professor at a British university, inviting him/her to organize a team for the international innovation contest to be held at your university. You should write about 100 words on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not use your own name. Use “Li Ming” instead. (10 points)","points":10,"image":null,"model":{"essay":"Dear Professor Smith, I am Li Ming, a student from Peking University. I am writing to invite you to organize a team to take part in the International Innovation Contest, which will be held at our university. The contest, themed \"Technology for a Better Future,\" will take place from July 15 to July 18 on our campus. It offers a wonderful chance for young talents worldwide to exchange creative ideas and showcase their projects. Your team's participation would surely make the event more inspiring. We would be honored if you could accept our invitation. I am looking forward to your reply. Yours sincerely, Li Ming","translation":"尊敬的史密斯教授：\n我是李明，北京大学的一名学生。我写此信是想邀请您组建一支团队，参加将在我校举办的国际创新大赛。\n本次大赛以‘科技创造美好未来’为主题，将于 7 月 15 日至 18 日在我校校园举行。它为全球青年人才提供了一个交流创意、展示项目的绝佳机会。您团队的参与必将使活动更富启发性。\n若蒙您接受邀请，我们将深感荣幸。期待您的回复。\n此致 李明","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"报家门＋直陈来意","text":"【第 1 段】 报家门＋直陈来意 。开头 I am Li Ming, a student from Peking University 先报身份，紧接 I am writing to invite you to organize a team… 一句点明邀请目的，落实 outline 第一块‘称呼＋亮明邀请’，正对须覆盖要点‘说明写信目的’。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"硬信息＋软价值","text":"【第 2 段】 硬信息＋软价值 。先 themed \"Technology for a Better Future\"、from July 15 to July 18、on our campus 给全主题、时间、地点；再 offers a wonderful chance… 点明交流展示的意义，完整兑现 outline 第二块与‘提供大赛相关信息（含参赛意义）’。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"敬语＋结束语＋落款","text":"【第 3 段】 敬语＋结束语＋落款 。We would be honored if you could accept our invitation 拉满诚意，I am looking forward to your reply 表期待，末尾 Yours sincerely 与 Li Ming 补齐邮件格式与署名，落实 outline 第三块及署名硬性要求。"}],"wordCount":104}},{"id":"section3-part-b","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part B 写作","directions":"52. Directions: Write an essay of 160-200 words based on the picture below. In your essay, you should 1) describe the picture briefly, 2) explain its intended meaning, and 3) give your comments. You should write neatly on the ANSWER SHEET. ( 20 points)","prompt":"52. Directions: Write an essay of 160-200 words based on the picture below. In your essay, you should 1) describe the picture briefly, 2) explain its intended meaning, and 3) give your comments. You should write neatly on the ANSWER SHEET. ( 20 points)","points":10,"image":{"src":"assets/writing/2022-english-one-section3-part-b.jpg","alt":"这是一幅手绘风格的黑白图片","width":"1024","height":"1024","local":"assets/writing/2022-english-one-section3-part-b.jpg"},"model":{"essay":"As is vividly depicted in the drawing, two female students in school uniforms stand before a bulletin board on which a poster announcing a campus lecture is pinned. The short-haired girl on the left remarks that since the lecture falls outside their major, attending it would be useless, while her long-haired companion insists that going to listen is bound to do them good. Simple as it appears, the drawing conveys a thought-provoking message about our attitude toward learning. What the girl on the left represents is a narrow, utilitarian mindset that dismisses any knowledge beyond one's own field, whereas the girl on the right symbolizes an open, curious spirit that welcomes broader horizons. In an age where disciplines increasingly overlap, the ability to absorb ideas across fields often sparks creativity and equips us for an uncertain future. In my view, the second girl's attitude deserves our applause. Knowledge is never confined to a single major; a lecture in an unfamiliar area may plant a seed that flourishes years later. Therefore, we young people should step out of our comfort zone and embrace every chance to learn.","translation":"正如图画生动呈现的那样，两名身着校服的女生站在一块布告栏前，栏上贴着一张宣布校园讲座的通知。左边的短发女生说，这场讲座不是她们的专业，去听也没用；而她那位长发的同伴则坚持认为，去听听肯定有好处。\n画面看似简单，却传递出一个发人深省的信息，关乎我们对待学习的态度。左边的女生代表着一种狭隘、功利的心态，对本专业之外的一切知识不屑一顾；右边的女生则象征着一种开放、好奇的精神，乐于拓宽视野。在一个学科日益交叉的时代，跨领域吸收观点的能力往往能激发创造力，让我们从容应对不确定的未来。\n在我看来，第二个女生的态度值得称赞。知识从不局限于单一专业；一场陌生领域的讲座，或许会埋下一颗多年后开花结果的种子。因此，我们年轻人应当走出舒适区，抓住每一次学习的机会。","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"客观白描落地描述任务","text":"【第 1 段】 客观白描落地描述任务 。本段用 As is vividly depicted 总起，再以 remarks that / insists that 如实转述两人相反的话，只写画面事实不下判断，落实了描述段客观白描的方法，完成 describe the picture briefly。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"象征动词点破核心寓意","text":"【第 2 段】 象征动词点破核心寓意 。本段以 Simple as it appears 过渡，用 What ... represents is / symbolizes 对比句架点出两种学习观，末句放到学科交叉的时代背景升华，写足了寓意这一得分核心，完成 explain its intended meaning。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"表态加对策首尾呼应","text":"【第 3 段】 表态加对策首尾呼应 。本段以 In my view 明确表态、给出理由，末句 Therefore 引出走出舒适区的呼吁回扣寓意，落实了亮态度加对策的方法，完成 give your comments。"}],"wordCount":186}}],"questionCount":45,"objectiveCount":45,"answerTable":{"ans-section1":"完形填空（四选一） Section I （1–20 题） 1 A coined · 2 C compared · 3 D Though · 4 C hinted at · 5 D differs · 6 B evidence · 7 C argued · 8 B forming · 9 A analogous · 10 D even · 11 C perspective · 12 B reducing · 13 A However · 14 C superficially · 15 B level · 16 D added · 17 A chances · 18 A danger · 19 D recognizes · 20 B poor \n（逐题解析见 完形填空（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-1":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (1) （21–25 题） 21 A · 22 C · 23 D · 24 D · 25 B \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-2":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (2) （26–30 题） 26 C · 27 B · 28 C · 29 D · 30 A \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-3":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (3) （31–35 题） 31 B · 32 A · 33 A · 34 B · 35 C \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-4":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (4) （36–40 题） 36 D · 37 A · 38 D · 39 B · 40 C \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-b":"段落小标题（7选5） Section II Part B （41–45 题） 41 F · 42 C · 43 A · 44 D · 45 G \n（逐题解析见 段落小标题（7选5）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-c":"英译汉（划线句子翻译） Section II Part C \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 英译汉（划线句子翻译）解析页","ans-section3-part-a":"写作 Section III Part A \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页","ans-section3-part-b":"写作 Section III Part B \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页"},"coverage":{"objective":45,"explained":45,"missingSections":[]}};
